---
import Case from '../../layouts/Case.astro';
import Summary from '../../components/Summary.astro';
import Row from '../../components/Row.astro';
import Decision from '../../components/Decision.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
import Note from '../../components/Note.astro';
---
<Case
  slug="borali"
  title="BorAli? Explore Brasília · Product Design Case Study · Beatriz Medina"
  description="Design of BorAli?, an app that helps people in Brasília decide what to do when they want to go out, with 12 interviews, fewer options by design and suggestions based on mood."
  kicker='<span class="idx">Case 04</span>iOS · 2026'
  lede="For people who want to go out but get stuck choosing what to do. The app starts from how they feel and offers a few curated options."
  chips={['12 interviews', 'Fewer options, faster decisions', 'User story mapping', 'UX Writing']}
  spec={[
    ['Role', 'Product Designer (UX/UI)'],
    ['Team', '5 people at the Apple Developer Academy'],
    ['Timeline', '7 weeks'],
    ['Platform', 'iOS (iPhone)'],
    ['Launch', 'May 2026'],
  ]}
  store={{ href: 'https://apps.apple.com/br/app/borali-explore-bras%C3%ADlia/id6764412373', label: 'App Store' }}
  cover={{ src: 'borali/screenshots.png', alt: 'BorAli? screens with mood picker, ready-made plans, exploration paths and place details in Brasília' }}
  toc={[
    { id: 'resumo', label: 'Summary' },
    { id: 'borali-pesquisa', label: 'Research' },
    { id: 'borali-ideacao', label: 'Decisions' },
    { id: 'borali-interface', label: 'Three paths' },
    { id: 'borali-validacao', label: 'Language' },
    { id: 'borali-resultado', label: 'Outcome' },
    { id: 'artefatos', label: 'Artifacts' },
  ]}
  next={{ href: '/en/sete.html', title: 'SETE', sub: 'iOS + watchOS · 2025' }}
>
  <section id="resumo" aria-labelledby="t-resumo">
    <h2 id="t-resumo" class="label">In 30 seconds</h2>
    <Summary>
      <Row label="Problem">Many people want to go out and explore the city, but put it off or give up when it’s time to pick a plan.</Row>
      <Row label="My role">Product Designer on a team of 5. I ran research and interviews, took part in the analysis and helped reframe the problem. There was no shortage of options, the hard part was deciding. Design and development decisions were made by the team.</Row>
      <Row label="Key decision">Limit choices on purpose, with one suggestion at a time based on mood and ready-made combos.</Row>
      <Row label="Outcome">Released on the App Store in May 2026.</Row>
    </Summary>
    <div class="prose lead-in" id="borali-estrategia">
      <p>The Academy challenge started broad, asking how an immersive experience could widen how we see the world. Studying urban spaces and how a city shapes behavior, the team arrived at a much more concrete problem, leaving the house.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="borali-pesquisa" class="block">
    <p class="label">Research · 12 interviews</p>
    <h2>There was no lack of options. There was too much effort in choosing.</h2>
    <div class="block-grid">
      <div class="prose">
        <p>I conducted interviews with the team, 12 in total, using a seven-question script that ranged from “Has it ever happened that you wanted to go out but ended up not going? Why?” to “Imagine you feel like going out right now, with no plan. What would you do first?”.</p>
        <p>Answers talked about laziness, tiredness, lack of company, money and indecision. One person described the ideal app as a place where they could say how they were feeling and get matching suggestions.</p>
      </div>
      <Note label="My contribution">In the analysis, I helped reframe the problem, from showing options to helping people decide. That finding shaped the idea of recommendations based on context and emotional state.</Note>
    </div>
    <ul class="quotes">
      <li><blockquote>“Muitas vezes vejo algum lugar que eu queria sair depois com os amigos, mas em meio a rotina corrida, acabo deixando pra depois e me esquecendo.”<span class="quote-tr" lang="en">I often see a place I’d like to go to with friends later, but with my busy routine I keep putting it off and forget.</span></blockquote><span>Interview 5</span></li>
      <li><blockquote>“Dá preguiça de procurar um novo lugar. Acho que assim eu teria uma recomendação menos enviesada do influencers”<span class="quote-tr" lang="en">Looking for a new place feels like a chore. I think this way I’d get a recommendation less biased by influencers.</span></blockquote><span>Interview 6</span></li>
      <li><blockquote>“Eu só queria saber o que tá acontecendo”<span class="quote-tr" lang="en">I just wanted to know what’s going on.</span></blockquote><span>Interview 9</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="aside-line">Interviews were conducted in Portuguese. Quotes are kept in the original, with an English translation below each.</p>
    <Figure src="borali/entrevistas.png" alt="Synthesis of the 12 interviews, each participant color-coded by the matching persona" caption="The 12 interviews, color-coded by the persona each person most resembled (in Portuguese)." n={1} crop />

    <div class="sub-block" id="borali-personas">
      <h3>Two ways of getting stuck</h3>
      <p class="sub-lede">Grouping the interviews surfaced two personas. Amanda, the tired explorer, has an intense routine and wants experiences ready to go, so her problem is deciding. Wallace, the stuck socializer, depends on invitations and keeps going to the same places, so his problem is discovering.</p>
      <Gallery items={[
        { src: 'borali/persona-1.png', alt: 'Persona Amanda, the tired explorer, established professional aged 28 to 40', caption: 'Amanda, the tired explorer' },
        { src: 'borali/persona-2.png', alt: 'Persona Wallace, the stuck socializer, university student aged 18 to 26', caption: 'Wallace, the stuck socializer' },
      ]} />
    </div>
    <p class="aside-line">The team’s synthesis also recorded behavioral hypotheses that guided the concept. People go out to feel something, not for the place itself; staying home makes you want to stay home; and guided discovery tends to work better than open-ended discovery.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="borali-ideacao" class="block">
    <p class="label">Decisions</p>
    <h2>Fewer options, on purpose.</h2>
    <p class="sub-lede decision-intro">Building on the reframed problem, the team defined the concept and the format of the suggestions.</p>
    <Decision n="01" title="The concept" id="d1">
      <Row label="Options">A real-time map of how busy places are, with reviews from people there; a catalog of trial classes and activities; and a map of Brasília attractions for planning group outings.</Row>
      <Row label="Choice" choice>An experience guided by the person’s moment, considering how they want to feel, who they’re with and what they want to do.</Row>
      <Row label="Why">All three concepts still asked people to compare options. That’s exactly where the interviews showed the friction.</Row>
    </Decision>
    <div class="decision-gap"></div>
    <Decision n="02" title="How many options to show" id="d2">
      <Row label="Basis">One of the user stories that guided prioritization read “As someone who feels overwhelmed by too many choices, I want to see a few well-selected options, so I can decide quickly without getting stuck.”</Row>
      <Row label="Choice" choice>In the story map, the personalized suggestion shows one at a time, with up to three swaps. There are three daily combos, with one swap.</Row>
      <Row label="Why">A clear limit nudges people toward a decision. Anyone who likes none of the options still has Explore, without the home screen turning into an endless list.</Row>
      <Figure slot="figure" src="borali/mapeamento.png" alt="User story map with three activities, find a personalized outing, pick a ready-made combo and explore places without a list" caption="User story map with the three paths and where each can end in a decision or a drop-off (in Portuguese)." n={2} crop />
    </Decision>
    <p class="aside-line">In the impact/effort matrix, sharing mood and seeing few options landed as high impact, high effort; a complete plan, group decisions, saved places and trustworthy reviews came in as high impact, low effort.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="borali-interface" class="block">
    <p class="label">Three paths to a decision</p>
    <h2>By mood, by a ready-made combo or exploring your own way.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The released version organizes the decision into three entry points. On the home screen, people say how they feel, by icon or by text, and pick a region. Right below are combos of two outings already put together. Explore offers four paths, Para mim (For me), Fora da bolha (Outside the bubble), Surpresa (Surprise) and Aqui perto (Nearby).</p>
    </div>
    <Gallery cols={4} phone items={[
      { src: 'borali/hi-01-inicio.png', alt: 'Home screen with mood picker by icon or text, region and combos', caption: 'Mood and combos' },
      { src: 'borali/hi-03-explorar-a.png', alt: 'Explore with four paths, For me, Outside the bubble, Surprise and Nearby', caption: 'Explore your way' },
      { src: 'borali/hi-04-explorar-b.png', alt: 'Place exploration screen', caption: 'Exploration' },
      { src: 'borali/hi-02-detalhes.png', alt: 'Place details with photos, location and reviews', caption: 'Place details' },
    ]} />
    <div class="sub-block">
      <h3>Visual foundation</h3>
      <p class="sub-lede">Colors and typography were set up as variables in Figma before the final screens, which made the interfaces easier to evolve and to hand off to development.</p>
      <div class="compare">
        <Figure src="borali/var-cores.png" alt="BorAli? color variables" caption="Color variables." crop />
        <Figure src="borali/var-tipografia.png" alt="BorAli? typography variables" caption="Typography variables." crop />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="borali-validacao" class="block">
    <p class="label">Language</p>
    <h2>From a formal question to a friend’s invitation.</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>From mid to high fidelity, the home screen structure stayed almost the same. What changed was the tone. The copy started to sound like a friend asking you out. The app is in Portuguese; translations are in parentheses.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare compare-phone">
      <Figure src="borali/mid-01-inicio.png" alt="Mid-fidelity home screen with the copy Olá Amanda, Como você se sente and Encontrar" tag="Mid-fidelity" />
      <Figure src="borali/hi-01-inicio.png" alt="High-fidelity home screen with the copy E aí, Hoje eu tô and Ver sugestões" tag="High-fidelity" after />
    </div>
    <dl class="changes">
      <Row label="Greeting">“Olá, Amanda” (Hello, Amanda) → “E aí” (Hey)</Row>
      <Row label="Question">“Como você se sente?” (How do you feel?) → “Hoje eu tô…” (Today I’m…)</Row>
      <Row label="Region">“Sem preferência” (No preference) → “Qualquer canto” (Anywhere)</Row>
      <Row label="Action">“Encontrar” (Find) → “Ver sugestões” (See suggestions)</Row>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section id="borali-resultado" class="block">
    <p class="label">Outcome</p>
    <h2>Released on the App Store in May 2026.</h2>
    <Summary plain>
      <Row label="Shipped">Suggestions based on mood, moment and context, ready-made combos, exploration by paths and saved places. The current version, 1.4.1, is from May 7, 2026.</Row>
      <Row label="What I learned">I used to look to research for answers that confirmed my ideas. Now I use discovery to challenge my assumptions and let the findings guide decisions.</Row>
      <Row label="Team">Ana Luísa Coleone, Beatriz Medina, Enzo Henrique, Lizandra Malta and Vinícius de Oliveira, mentored by Júlio César and Felipe Carvalho.</Row>
    </Summary>
  </section>

  <section id="artefatos" class="block">
    <p class="label">Process artifacts</p>
    <h2>Journey, prioritization and benchmarking.</h2>
    <details class="artifacts" id="borali-jornada">
      <summary>User journey</summary>
      <Figure src="borali/jornada.png" alt="Amanda’s journey from searching to the app’s response" caption="Amanda’s journey, from searching to the app’s response (in Portuguese)." />
    </details>
    <details class="artifacts" id="borali-priorizacao">
      <summary>User stories and prioritization</summary>
      <Gallery items={[
        { src: 'borali/historias.png', alt: 'Eleven BorAli? user stories', caption: 'User stories' },
        { src: 'borali/matriz.png', alt: 'Impact/effort matrix with the user stories', caption: 'Impact/effort matrix' },
      ]} />
    </details>
    <details class="artifacts" id="borali-benchmarking">
      <summary>Competitive analysis</summary>
      <Figure src="borali/benchmarking.png" alt="Competitive analysis of place and event discovery apps" caption="Competitive analysis." />
    </details>
    <details class="artifacts" id="borali-telas">
      <summary>More screens</summary>
      <Gallery cols={3} phone items={[
        { src: 'borali/mid-02-detalhes.png', alt: 'Mid-fidelity place details', caption: 'Details, mid-fidelity' },
        { src: 'borali/hi-overview.png', alt: 'High-fidelity prototype overview', caption: 'Overview' },
      ]} />
    </details>
  </section>
</Case>
